== Chapter X

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #text(size: 0.9em)[The deceit of sin in drawing the mind away from attending to particular duties further uncovered. Several things required in the mind of believers with regard to particular duties of obedience. The actings of sin by way of deceit to divert the mind from them.]
]

#smallcaps[We] have not yet brought to a conclusion the #emph[first way] in which the deceit of sin works, that is, in #emph[drawing the mind away] from carrying out its duty. We dwell on it the longer for two reasons.

First, because of its importance and the concern it carries. If the mind is drawn away, if it is tainted, weakened, or turned aside from a proper and strict attending to its charge and office, then the whole soul, will, and affections are certainly entangled and drawn into sin, as has been shown in part and will appear further later. So we ought to give diligent heed to this, which is the design of the apostle’s exhortation in Heb 2:1: “We must pay closer attention, therefore, to what we have heard, so that we do not drift away” from it. What he warns us against is a failure of our minds, through the deceitfulness of sin, by which we lose the life, power, sense, and impression of the #emph[word.] And there is no way to prevent it except by paying the #emph[closest attention] to what we have heard, which expresses the whole duty of our minds in attending to obedience.

Secondly, because the actings and workings of the #emph[mind] are spiritual, and so the #emph[conscience] is not affected by them so as to take proper notice of them, unless it is clearly enlightened and properly roused and stirred up. Conscience is not apt to turn back and act on the mind’s failures, since it chiefly regards the acts of the whole soul. When the #emph[affections] are entangled with sin (of which more later), or the will begins to conceive it by its express consent, #emph[conscience] is apt to raise an uproar in the soul and give it no rest or quiet until the soul is reclaimed, or until conscience itself is in one way or another bribed or corrupted. But these neglects of the mind are #emph[spiritual,] and without very diligent attention they are seldom noticed. In the Scriptures our #emph[minds] are often called our #emph[spirits,] as in Rom 1:9: “whom I serve with my spirit.” And they are distinguished from the #emph[soul,] which chiefly means the affections, in the division made in 1 Thess 5:23: may he sanctify you completely, “your entire spirit and soul,” that is, your mind and affections. It is true that where #emph[spirit] is used to express #emph[spiritual] gifts, it is set against our #emph[understanding] with regard to those gifts (1 Cor 14:15), where understanding is taken for the first act of the mind in a rational perception of things. But when that word is applied to any faculty of our souls, it is the #emph[mind] that it expresses. Since this, then, is #emph[our spirit,] its actings are secret and hidden, and cannot be discovered without spiritual wisdom and diligence. Let us not suppose, then, that we dwell too long on this consideration. It is of such great importance to us, and yet so hidden, and we are apt to be so unaware of it; and yet our carefulness in this matter is one of the best evidences we have of our sincerity. Let us not be like a man who feels and complains of a cut finger, but not of a failing of his vital spirits that is leading to death. So under this head of our discourse there remains the consideration of the charge of the mind with regard to particular duties and sins. In considering it we will do these two things.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, show what is required in the #emph[mind of a believer] with regard to particular duties.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, declare the way the deceit of sin works to #emph[draw] the mind away from attending to them. The same will be done also with regard to particular sins and avoiding them.]]

=== What the Mind Is to Attend unto in Duties

First, for the right performance of any duty it is not enough that the thing required is performed. It must also be squared and fitted in every respect to its rule. Here lies the great #emph[duty] of the #emph[mind:] to attend to the #emph[rule] of duties, and to take care that everything concerning them is ordered by it. Our progress in obedience is our #emph[edification,] or building. Now it does very little to further a #emph[building] for a man to bring wood and stones and heap them up together without order. They must be hewn and squared and fitted by line and rule if we intend to build. Nor is it of any advantage to our #emph[edification] in faith and obedience to multiply duties, if we heap them on one another and do not order and arrange them according to rule. That is why God expressly rejects a #emph[multitude of duties] when they are not suited in every respect to the rule. Isa 1:11: “What good to Me is your multitude of sacrifices?” and verse 14: “They have become a burden to Me; I am weary of bearing them.” And so all acceptable obedience is called walking according to a rule (Gal 6:16). It is #emph[canonical,] that is, regular, obedience, obedience by rule. As #emph[letters of the alphabet] heaped together mean nothing unless they are arranged in their proper order, so neither do our duties mean anything without this arranging. To see that they are arranged so is the great duty of the mind, and it is to attend to it with all diligence. Eph 5:15: #emph[Walk circumspectly,] exactly, accurately, that is, diligently in all things; take heed to the rule of what you do. We #emph[walk] in duties, but we walk #emph[circumspectly] in this attention of the mind.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, there are some special things the rule directs to, which the mind is to attend to in every duty, such as:]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, that the duty is full and complete in its #emph[matter.] Under the law no beast that lacked any member, that had any part missing, was allowed as a sacrifice. Such beasts were rejected as well as those that were #emph[lame] or #emph[blind.] Duties must be complete in their parts, their matter. Part of the price may be kept back in such a way as to make the offering of all the rest unacceptable. #emph[Saul’s] sparing of #emph[Agag] and the fattest of the cattle made the destroying of all the rest useless. So when men will #emph[give alms,] or perform other services, but not in the proportion the rule requires, and which the mind might discover by diligently attending to it, the whole duty is spoiled.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, as to its #emph[principle:] that it is done in faith, and so by actually drawing strength from Christ (John 15:5), without whom “we can do nothing.” It is not enough that the person is a #emph[believer,] though that is necessary for every good work (Eph 2:10). Faith must also be particularly exercised in every duty we do. For our whole obedience is the “obedience of faith” (Rom 1:5), that is, the obedience the doctrine of faith requires, and which the grace of faith bears or brings forth. So Christ is expressly said to be “our life” (Col 3:4), our spiritual life, that is, its spring, author, and cause. Now as in natural life no vital act can be performed except by the actual working of the principle of life itself, so in spiritual life no spiritually vital act, that is, no duty acceptable to God, can be performed except by the actual working of Christ, who is our life. And this is passed on to us in no other way than by faith. So the apostle says in Gal 2:20: “Christ lives in me. The life I now live in the flesh, I live by the faith of the Son of God.” Not only was Christ his life, a living principle in him, but he #emph[lived a life,] that is, he performed vital actions in all duties of holiness and obedience, by the faith of the Son of God, or faith in him, drawing supplies of grace and strength from him by it. So this is what a believer ought diligently to attend to: that everything he does toward God is done in the strength of Christ. And what that consists in ought to be diligently inquired into by all who intend to walk with God.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, in this regard to the rule, the #emph[manner] of performing every duty is to be considered. Now there are two things in the manner of performing any duty that a believer, who is entrusted with spiritual light, ought to attend to.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, that it is done #emph[in the way] and by the means God has prescribed for the outward manner of its performance. This is especially to be regarded in duties of the worship of God, in which the matter and the outward manner both fall equally under his command. If this is not regarded, the whole duty is spoiled. I do not speak of those who let themselves be deluded by the #emph[deceitfulness of sin] into utterly disregarding the rule of the word in such things and worshiping God according to their own imaginations. I speak chiefly of those who in general profess to do nothing except #emph[what] God requires, and #emph[as] he requires it, and yet do not diligently attend to the rule, so as to make the authority of God the sole cause and reason both of what they do and of the #emph[manner] in which they do it. And this is the reason God so often calls on his people to #emph[consider diligently] and wisely, so that they may do everything as he has commanded.]]

#pad(left: 6.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, the affections of the heart and mind in duties belong to the inward manner of performing them. The prescriptions and commands of God to attend to this are countless, and the lack of it makes every duty an abomination to him. A #emph[sacrifice] without a heart, without salt, without fire, what is it worth? No more are duties worth anything without spiritual affections. Here the mind is to keep the charge of God, to see that the heart he requires is offered to him. And we find also that God requires special affections to go with special duties: “he who gives, with cheerfulness.” If these are not attended to, the whole is lost.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fourthly, the mind is to attend to the #emph[ends] of duties, and chiefly among them the glory of God in Christ. Sin and self will impose several other #emph[ends] on our duties. There are two especially that sin will press hard on us: first, #emph[the satisfying of our convictions] and consciences; secondly, #emph[the praise of men.] For self-righteousness and display are the main ends of men who have fallen away from God, in all moral duties whatever. In their sins they try to satisfy their lusts; in their duties, their convictions and their pride. The mind of a believer is to watch diligently against these, and to keep up in everything a single eye to the glory of God, as what answers to the great and general rule of all our obedience: “whatever you#super[+] do, do it all to the glory of God.” These things and others like them, I say, which are commonly spoken of, the mind of a believer is bound to attend to diligently and constantly with regard to all the particular duties of our walking before God. Here, then, lies no small part of the deceit of sin: to #emph[draw the mind away] from this watch, and bring an inattention on it, so that in these things it does not keep the watch and charge of the Lord. If sin can do this, and so strip our duties of all their excellence, which lies in these aspects of them that the mind is to attend to, it will not trouble itself or us much about the duties themselves. And it attempts this in several ways.]]

=== How Sin Draws the Mind Off from Duties

First, by persuading the #emph[mind] to content itself with #emph[generalities,] and drawing it away from attending to things in particular instances. For example, it would persuade the soul to rest satisfied in a #emph[general aim] of doing things to the glory of God, without considering how every particular duty may tend that way. So #emph[Saul] thought he had fulfilled his whole duty, done the will of God, and sought his glory in his war against #emph[Amalek.] Yet for lack of attending to every particular duty in that service, he had dishonored God and ruined himself and his descendants. And men may persuade themselves that they have a general design for the #emph[glory of God] when they have no active principle in particular duties tending that way at all. But if, instead of fixing the mind by faith on particularly advancing the glory of God in a duty, the soul contents itself with a #emph[general notion] of doing so, the mind is already diverted and drawn away from its charge by the deceitfulness of sin. If a man is traveling on a journey, it is not only required of him that he turns his course that way and keeps going. If he does not attend to every turning and the other things he meets on his way, he may wander and never come to his journey’s end. So suppose that in #emph[general] we aim at the glory of God, as we all profess to do. Yet if we do not attend to it distinctly in every duty that meets us on our way, we will never attain the #emph[end] we aim at. And he who satisfies himself with this #emph[general] purpose, without acting on it in every #emph[special] duty, will not keep that purpose for long either. Sin does the same work on the mind with regard to the #emph[principle] of our duties as it does with regard to their end. Their #emph[principle] is that they are done in faith, in the strength of Christ. But if men content themselves that they are #emph[believers,] that they have faith, and do not labor in every particular duty to exercise faith, to lead their spiritual lives in all the acts of those duties “by the faith of the Son of God,” then the mind is drawn away from its duty. It is in particular actions that we express and exercise our faith and obedience; and what we are in them, that we are, and no more.

Secondly, it draws the #emph[mind] away from the duties already mentioned by slipping into it a secret #emph[contentment] with the duty itself as performed, as to its matter. This is a fair discharge for a natural conscience. If the duty is performed, conscience and conviction will be satisfied, even though in the #emph[manner] of its performance it falls short of the rule in almost everything. So #emph[Saul,] after his expedition against #emph[Amalek,] cries to #emph[Samuel]: “May the #smallcaps[Lord] bless you. I have carried out the #smallcaps[Lord]’s instructions.” He satisfied himself, though he had not attended as he ought to the whole will of God in that matter. So it was with those in Isa 58:3. “Why have we fasted,” they say, “and You have not seen?” They had pleased themselves in performing their duties, and expected that God also would be pleased with them. But he shows them at length where they had failed, and that so far as to make what they had done an abomination. He makes the same charge against them in 48:1, 2. This is what the deceitfulness of sin tries to bring the mind to: to rest in the performance of the duty itself. “You ought to pray, and you have prayed. You ought to give alms, and you have given alms. Then be at ease in what you have done, and go on to do the same.” If it prevails in this, the #emph[mind] is released from further attending and watching over duty, and this leaves the soul on the borders of many evils. For:

Thirdly, from this #emph[mere routine] in all duties will quickly follow, which is the height of sin’s drawing the mind away from duty. For men’s minds may be drawn away from all duties in the midst of performing them most abundantly. In and under them the mind may be subject to a #emph[habitual] diversion from its charge and its watch over the rule. What is done in such a frame is not done to God (Amos 5:25). None of their sacrifices were to God, though they professed that all of them were. But they did not attend to his worship in faith and to his glory, and he despised all their duties. See also Hos 10:1. This is the great reason why #emph[professors] thrive so little under the performance of a multitude of duties. They do not attend to them in a #emph[proper manner,] since their minds have been drawn away from their circumspect watch. So they have little or no communion with God in them, which is the end they are designed for, and the only thing by which they become useful and profitable to them. In this way many duties of worship and obedience are performed by a woeful generation of #emph[hypocrites,] formalists, and profane persons, without either life or light in themselves or acceptance with God, their minds having been wholly estranged from properly attending to what they do by the power and deceitfulness of sin.

=== What the Mind Is to Attend unto as to Sin

Secondly, as it is with regard to duties, so it is also with regard to #emph[sins.] There are various things in and about #emph[every sin] that the mind of a #emph[believer,] by virtue of its #emph[office] and duty, is bound to attend to diligently, to preserve the soul from it. They are things God has appointed and sanctified to give effectual rebukes and checks to the whole working of the law of sin; and in the law of grace, under which we are, they are exceedingly suited and fitted for that purpose. The #emph[deceit of sin] tries by all means to draw the mind away from properly considering and attending to these. We will reflect a little on a few of them.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, the first and most general is the #emph[sovereignty] of God, the great lawgiver, by whom sin is forbidden. #emph[Joseph] fixed on this in his great temptation (Gen 39:9): “How could I do such a great evil and sin against God?” There was a great evil in it, a great ingratitude against man, which he also pleads and insists on (vv. 8, 9). But what fixed his heart and resolution against it was its #emph[formal nature,] that it was #emph[sin against God,] by whom it was severely forbidden. So the apostle tells us that in our dealing in anything that is against the law, our regard is always to be to the #emph[lawgiver] and his sovereignty (Jas 4:11, 12): “If you judge the law, you are not a practitioner of the law, but a judge of it. There is only one Lawgiver, the One who is able to save and destroy.” Consider this always: there is one #emph[lawgiver,] holy, righteous, armed with sovereign power and authority, and he is able to save and to destroy. That is why sin is called #emph[rebellion,] a casting off of his yoke, a despising of him, and that in his sovereignty as the great lawgiver. The mind ought always to attend to this in practice, in all the lustings, actings, and suggestions of the law of sin, especially when it is helped by some suitable or vigorous temptation: “It is #emph[God who has forbidden this thing,] the great lawgiver, under whose absolute sovereignty I am, on whom I depend for my life, and by whom I am to be disposed of in my present and eternal condition.” #emph[Eve] fixed on this at the beginning of her temptation: “God has said, ‘You must not eat of it’” (Gen 3:3). But she did not hold her ground. She did not stay with that consideration, but let her mind be diverted from it by the subtlety of #emph[Satan,] and this was the entrance of her transgression. And so it is with all of us in our straying from obedience.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, the #emph[deceit of sin,] of every sin, the #emph[punishment] appointed for it in the law, is another thing the mind ought actually to attend to with regard to every particular evil. And the diversions from this that the minds of men have suffered, in doctrine and in practice, have been an inlet to every kind of abomination. #emph[Job] professes another frame in himself in 31:23: “For destruction from God terrifies me, and because of His majesty I could not endure.” In the preceding verses he had mentioned many evils and pleads his innocence of them, though they were such that, on account of his #emph[greatness] and power, he could easily have committed them without fear of danger from men. Here he gives the reason that prevailed with him to abstain from them so carefully: “destruction from God terrifies me, and because of His majesty I could not endure.” “I considered,” he says, “that God had appointed death and destruction as the #emph[punishment] of sin, and that his greatness, majesty, and power were such that he could inflict it to the uttermost, in a way no creature is able to bear or to avoid.” So the apostle directs believers always to consider that “it is a fearful thing to fall into the hands of the living God” (Heb 10:31). And this is because he has said, “Vengeance is Mine; I will repay” (v. 30). He is a #emph[sin-avenging God,] who will by no means acquit the guilty. So in the declaration of his gracious name, which is infinitely full of encouragements to poor sinners in Christ, he adds this at the close: “He will by no means leave the guilty unpunished” (Exod 34:7). He does this so that he may keep on the minds of those he pardons a proper sense of the punishment due from his #emph[avenging justice] to every sin. So the apostle would have us bear in mind that even our “God is a consuming fire” (Heb 12:29), that is, that we should consider his holiness and avenging justice, which appoints for sin a fitting recompense of reward. And he counts men’s breaking through this consideration as the height of the aggravation of their sins (Rom 1:32): “Although they know God’s righteous decree that those who do such things are worthy of death, they … continue to do these things.” What hope is there for such persons? There is indeed relief against this consideration in the blood of Christ for humbled, believing souls. But this relief is not meant to #emph[draw the mind away] from it, as God has appointed it to be a restraint from sin. And both these considerations, the #emph[sovereignty] of God and the #emph[punishment of sin,] are put together by our Savior in Matt 10:28: “Do not be afraid of those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul. Instead, fear the One who can destroy both soul and body in hell.”]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, the consideration of all the #emph[love and kindness] of God, against whom every sin is committed, is another thing the mind ought diligently to attend to. And this is a prevailing consideration, if it is rightly and graciously managed in the soul. #emph[Moses] presses it on the people in Deut 32:6: “Is this how you#super[+] repay the #smallcaps[Lord], O foolish and senseless people? Is He not your Father, who bought you? Has He not made you and established you?” Is this the return for #emph[eternal love] and all its fruits, for the love and care of a Father, of a Redeemer, that we have been made partakers of? And it is the same consideration the #emph[apostle] uses to this purpose in 2 Cor 7:1: “Therefore, beloved, since we have these promises, let us cleanse ourselves from everything that defiles body and spirit, perfecting holiness in the fear of God.” The receiving of the promises ought to be effectual both to stir us up to all holiness and to work and bring about an abstaining from all sin. And what promises are these? That God will “be a Father to us, and receive us” (6:17, 18), which takes in the whole of all the love of God toward us, here and to eternity. If there is any spiritual ingenuousness in the soul, then while the mind is attentive to this consideration, no prevailing attempt can be made on it by the power of sin. Now there are two parts to this consideration.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, what is general in it, what is common to all believers. This is used to this purpose in 1 John 3:1, 2, 3: “Behold what manner of love the Father has given to us, that we should be called children of God. And that is what we are! The reason the world does not know us is that it did not know Him. Beloved, we are now children of God, and what we will be has not yet been revealed. We know that when Christ appears, we will be like Him, for we will see Him as He is. And everyone who has this hope in Him purifies himself, just as Christ is pure.” Consider, he says, the love of God, and the privileges we enjoy by it: “Behold what manner of love the Father has given to us, that we should be called children of God.” #emph[Adoption] is a special fruit of it, and how great a privilege this is! Such is this love, and such are its fruits, that the world knows nothing of the blessed condition we obtain and enjoy by it: “the world does not know us.” Indeed, it is such love, and its effects are so unspeakably blessed and glorious, that we ourselves are not able to comprehend them. What use, then, ought we to make of this contemplation of the excellent, unspeakable love of God? Why, he says, “everyone who has this hope purifies himself.” Every man who has been made partaker of this love, and has from it a #emph[hope] of fully enjoying its fruits, of being made like God in glory, #emph[purifies himself,] that is, by abstaining from each and every sin, as is declared at length in the words that follow.]]

#pad(left: 4.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, it is to be considered with regard to the #emph[particular mercies] and fruits of love that #emph[each one’s soul] has been made partaker of. There is no #emph[believer] who does not, besides the love and mercy he has in common with all his brethren, also have in the lot of his inheritance some #emph[enclosures,] some special mercies in which he has a property of his own. He has some #emph[joy] that no stranger shares in (Prov 14:10), particular applications of covenant love and mercy to his soul. Now these are all provisions God has laid in so that they may be kept in mind against an hour of temptation, and so that the consideration of them may preserve the soul from the attempts of sin. To neglect them greatly aggravates our provocations (1 Kgs 11:9). It is charged as the great evil of #emph[Solomon] that he had sinned against special mercies, special intimations of love: he sinned after God had appeared to him twice. God required that he should have kept that #emph[special favor] in mind and made it an argument against sin. But he neglected it, and is burdened with this severe rebuke. And indeed all special mercies, all special tokens and pledges of love, are utterly lost and wasted on us if they are not improved to this end. This, then, is another thing that it is the duty of the mind to attend to greatly, and to set effectually against every attempt the law of sin makes on the soul.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fourthly, the considerations that arise from the blood and mediation of Christ are of the same importance. So the #emph[apostle] declares in 2 Cor 5:14, 15: “For Christ’s love constrains us, because we are convinced that One died for all, therefore all died. And He died for all, that those who live should no longer live for themselves, but for Him who died for them and was raised again.” There is a constraining efficacy in this consideration. It is great, forceful, and effectual, if it is properly attended to. But I must not dwell on these things in particular here. Nor,]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fifthly, will I speak of the #emph[indwelling of the Spirit,] the greatest privilege we are made partakers of in this world. The proper consideration of how he is grieved by sin, how his #emph[dwelling place] is defiled by it, and how his comforts are forfeited, lost, and despised by it, might also be dwelt on. But the instances gone through are sufficient for our purpose. Now here lies the duty of the #emph[mind] with regard to particular sins and temptations. It is to attend diligently and carefully to these things, to dwell constantly on the consideration of them, and to have them always ready to set against all the lustings, actings, warrings, attempts, and rage of sin.]]

=== How Sin Draws the Mind Off as to Sin

With regard to this, #emph[sin] puts forth and acts its #emph[deceit] in a special way. It labors by all means to draw the mind away from its #emph[proper attending] to these things, and so to rob the soul of this great preservative and antidote against its poison. It tries to make the soul satisfy itself with general, #emph[undigested notions about sin,] so that it may have nothing in particular to turn to in its own defense against sin’s attempts and temptations. The ways it does this may also be briefly considered.

First, it is from the deceit of sin that the mind is #emph[spiritually slothful,] and by this it becomes negligent of this duty. The chief way the mind carries out its trust in this matter is expressed by #emph[watching,] which is the great warning the Lord #emph[Jesus] gave his disciples with regard to all their dangers from sin and Satan (Mark 13:37): “What I say to you, I say to everyone: Keep watch!” That is, use your utmost diligence and circumspection, so that you#super[+] are not surprised and entangled by temptations. It is called also #emph[consideration:] “Consider your ways”\; “consider your latter end.” God complains of the lack of this in his people (Deut 32:29). Now what is contrary to these indispensable conditions of our preservation is #emph[spiritual slothfulness,] as the apostle declares in Heb 6:11, 12: “We want each of you#super[+] to show this same diligence to the very end, in order to make your hope sure. Then you#super[+] will not be slothful.” If we do not show diligence, we are slothful, and in danger of falling short of inheriting the promises. See 2 Pet 1:5, 9: “make every effort to add to your faith virtue; and to virtue, knowledge,” and so on. “For if you#super[+] possess these qualities and continue to grow in them, they will keep you#super[+] from being ineffective and unproductive in your knowledge of our Lord Jesus Christ. But whoever lacks these traits is nearsighted to the point of blindness, having forgotten that he has been cleansed from his past sins. Therefore, brothers, strive to make your calling and election sure. For if you#super[+] practice these things you#super[+] will never stumble, and you#super[+] will receive a lavish reception into the eternal kingdom of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ” (vv. 9, 10). The mind is turned from all this if once it is made #emph[slothful] by the deceit of sin. Now this sloth consists in three things.

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[First, #emph[inattention.] The mind does not set itself to consider and attend to what specially concerns it. The apostle, urging the #emph[Hebrews] with all earnestness to #emph[attend diligently] and consider carefully, so that they may not be hardened by the deceitfulness of sin, gives this reason for their danger: #emph[they had become dull of hearing] (Heb 5:11). That is, they were #emph[slothful,] and did not attend to the things of their duty. A secret #emph[carelessness] is apt to creep over the soul, and it does not set itself to mark diligently how things go with it and what is continually laid on it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, an #emph[unwillingness to be stirred up to its duty] (Prov 19:24): “The slothful man buries his hand in his bosom; he will not even bring it back to his mouth.” In #emph[sloth] there is an unwillingness to take any notice of warnings, calls, rousings, or stirrings up by the word, the Spirit, judgments, or anything God uses to call the mind to a proper consideration of the condition of the soul. And it is a perfect evidence that the mind has been made #emph[slothful] by the deceit of sin when special calls and warnings, whether in a suitable word or a pressing judgment, cannot prevail with it to pull its hand out of its bosom, that is, to set about the special duties it is called to.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Thirdly, #emph[weak and ineffectual attempts] to recover itself to its duty (Prov 26:14): “As a door turns on its hinges, so the slothful man turns on his bed.” In the turning of a door on its hinges there is some motion, but no progress. It moves back and forth, but it is still in the place and position it was in. So it is with the spiritually #emph[slothful man] on his bed, or in his security. He makes some motions or faint efforts toward carrying out his duty, but he does not go on. Where he was one day, there he is the next; indeed, where he was one year, there he is the next. His efforts are faint, cold, and fleeting. He gains no ground by them, but is always beginning his work and never finishing it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Fourthly, #emph[losing heart at the sight of difficulties] and discouragements (Prov 22:13): “The slothful man says, ‘There is a lion outside! I will be slain in the streets!’” Every difficulty deters him from duty. He thinks it impossible for him to attain the accuracy, exactness, and perfection he is to press after in this matter. So he contents himself with his old coldness and negligence rather than run the risk of a universal circumspection. Now if the deceit of sin has once #emph[drawn the mind away] into this frame, it lays it open to every temptation and invasion of sin. The #emph[bride in the Song of Songs] seems to have been overtaken by this disorder (5:1, 2, 3). It puts her on various #emph[excuses] for why she cannot attend to the call of Christ and apply herself to her duty of walking with him.]]

#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Secondly, it draws the mind away from its watch and duty with regard to sin by #emph[surprises.] It joins with some pressing temptation and #emph[surprises the mind] into thoughts of quite another nature than those it ought to dwell on in its own defense. So it seems to have been with #emph[Peter.] His carnal fear closed with the temptation in which Satan sought to sift him, and filled his mind with so many thoughts about his own imminent danger that he could not take into consideration the love and warning of Christ, or the evil his temptation led him to, or anything he ought to have dwelt on for his preservation. And so, on looking back over his folly in neglecting those thoughts of God and of the love of Christ, which with the help of the Holy Spirit might have kept him from his scandalous fall, he “wept bitterly.” And this indeed is the common way the #emph[deceit of sin] works with regard to particular evils. It lays hold on the mind #emph[suddenly] with thoughts about the present sin, possesses it, and takes it up. Either the mind does not recover itself at all to the considerations mentioned, or, if any thoughts of them are suggested, the mind is so taken up and filled beforehand that they make no impression on the soul or do not stay in it. This is doubtless how #emph[David] was surprised at the entrance of his great sin. Sin and temptation so #emph[possessed and filled his mind] with the present object of his lust that he utterly forgot, as it were, the considerations he had made use of before, when he kept himself so diligently from his iniquity. So here lies the great wisdom of the soul: rejecting the very first motions of sin. For by bargaining with them the mind may be drawn away from attending to its preservatives, and so the whole soul rushes into evil.]

Thirdly, it draws the mind away #emph[by the frequency and long continuance] of its solicitations, at last, as it were, conquering it. And this does not happen without an open neglect on the soul’s part, in failing to stir itself up to give sin an effectual rebuke in the strength and by the grace of Christ, which would have prevented its prevailing power. But more will be said of this later. This is the first way the law of sin acts its deceit against the soul. It #emph[draws the mind away] from attending to its charge and office, both with regard to duty and with regard to sin. And so far as this is done, the person is said to be #emph[drawn away, or drawn off.] He is tempted; each one is tempted #emph[when he is drawn away in this way] by his own lust, or by the deceit of sin dwelling in him. And the whole effect of this working of the deceitfulness of sin may be brought under these three heads.

First, the #emph[slackening of a universally watchful frame of spirit] toward every duty and against all the actings of sin, even the most hidden and secret.

Secondly, the omission of special attending to the duties that have a special regard to the weakening and ruin of the whole law of sin, and to heading off its deceitfulness.

Thirdly, spiritual sloth with regard to diligently attending to all the special concerns of duties and sins. When these three things, with the branches of them mentioned, are brought about in or on the soul, more or less, then so far as they are, a man is drawn away by his own lust, or by the deceit of sin.

There is no need to add here any directions for preventing this evil. They have been laid down sufficiently as we went through the consideration both of the #emph[duty] of the mind and of the #emph[deceit] of sin.
